CAT = "s63Iars"; SEG = "IAR"
Q = [
("Which employee of a state-registered adviser is an investment adviser representative?",
 "One who manages client portfolios", ["One who only books client meetings", "One who files client paperwork", "An outside auditor of the firm"],
 "An IAR makes recommendations, manages accounts, solicits or sells advisory services, or supervises those who do. Clerical and administrative staff are excluded, and an outside auditor is not employed by the adviser to give advice."),
("An IAR works for a federal covered adviser. A state may require her to register there only if she:",
 "Has a place of business there", ["Has any client living there", "Earns most of her pay there", "Is asked to by the SEC"],
 "Under NSMIA and SEC Rule 203A-3, states may register an IAR of a federal covered adviser only if the IAR has a place of business in that state. Having clients there is not enough."),
("Under NASAA's model rule on IAR registration, which qualification satisfies the competency requirement?",
 "Passing the Series 65", ["The Series 63 on its own", "The Series 7 on its own", "The SIE on its own"],
 "Model Rule 202(a)-2 accepts the Series 65, or the Series 66 together with the Series 7, or certain designations such as the CFP or CFA. The Series 63 alone qualifies an agent, not an IAR."),
("An individual is paid by an adviser to find and refer new advisory clients. Under the USA she is:",
 "An IAR of the adviser", ["Outside securities regulation", "A broker-dealer", "An issuer of securities"],
 "The IAR definition includes anyone who solicits, offers or negotiates for the sale of advisory services on the adviser's behalf. A paid solicitor must register as an IAR unless an exclusion applies."),
("A registered agent of a broker-dealer starts charging clients a separate fee for written financial plans. She:",
 "Must also register as an IAR", ["Is covered by agent registration", "Registers only past 15 clients", "Needs nothing if plans are written"],
 "A broker-dealer agent is excluded from the adviser definition only when advice is solely incidental and there is no special compensation. A separate fee for advice makes her an IAR who must register."),
("When an IAR of a state-registered adviser leaves the firm, who must promptly notify the Administrator?",
 "The investment adviser", ["The departing IAR alone", "The adviser's clients", "The SEC"],
 "For an adviser registered with the state, the adviser reports the start or end of an IAR's association. For an IAR of a federal covered adviser, the IAR herself notifies the state."),
("Under the rules followed by NASAA and the SEC, an adviser may charge a performance-based fee to:",
 "Qualified clients", ["Any client who signs", "Clients over age 65", "Clients with IRAs"],
 "Performance fees are generally prohibited except for qualified clients, who meet the asset or net worth tests in SEC Rule 205-3. Age or account type does not create an exception."),
("An IAR places a client's first discretionary trade after the client grants discretion by phone. NASAA's rule requires written authority within:",
 "10 business days", ["15 calendar days", "30 days", "24 hours"],
 "NASAA's unethical practices rule for advisers lets discretion begin on verbal authority but requires written discretionary authority within 10 business days after the first discretionary transaction."),
("An IAR's state registration is effective only while the individual:",
 "Is associated with an adviser", ["Holds a professional designation", "Keeps a personal surety bond", "Has a branch office in the state"],
 "IAR registration depends on association with a registered or federal covered adviser. When the association ends, the individual may not act as an IAR until associated again."),
("Under NASAA's model rule on unethical practices, which is unethical for an IAR?",
 "Charging an unreasonable fee", ["Billing a fee disclosed in Form ADV", "Offering a free first meeting", "Explaining risks in writing"],
 "Charging a client an unreasonable advisory fee is listed as unethical even when the fee is disclosed. Disclosed fees, free consultations and written risk explanations are proper."),
]
